 "intro": "Ireland punches far above its size in literature and music, and its festivals, from St Patrick's Day to Halloween, are celebrated worldwide.",
 "chapters": [
  {
   "id": "words",
   "title": "Writers",
   "text": [
    "Ireland has four Nobel Prize winners in Literature: W. B. Yeats, George Bernard Shaw, Samuel Beckett and Seamus Heaney. James Joyce, Oscar Wilde, Jonathan Swift and Bram Stoker, author of Dracula, were also Irish."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "music",
   "title": "Music and festivals",
   "text": [
    "Traditional music uses the fiddle, tin whistle, bodhrán drum and uilleann pipes. U2, The Cranberries and Hozier are famous Irish acts, and Ireland has won Eurovision seven times, a joint record.",
    "Halloween grew from the ancient Celtic festival of Samhain, which marked the start of winter."
   ]
  }
 ],
